    },
    "ocorrencias": [
      {
        "linha": 55,
        "tokens": [
          "1812515"
        ],
        "trecho": "Classical empirical models FSPL, Okumura--Hata, and COST\\,231 lose accuracy where elevation gradients, vegetation density, and urban fabric shape sub-kilometer RF propagation beyond coarse terrain categories. We propose GNN-RF, a heterogeneous graph neural network that couples GATv2 message passing with a physics-constrained decoder whose loss penalizes departures from free-space bounds, distance--attenuation monotonicity, variance preservation, and NDVI sign consistency. The model is trained on $12.96 \\times 10^6$-node terrain graphs with 18 topographic and spectral features from four S\\~{a}o"
      },
      {
        "linha": 287,
        "tokens": [
          "1812515"
        ],
        "trecho": "Total parameters & \\num{1812515} \\\\"
      },
      {
        "linha": 412,
        "tokens": [
          "265602",
          "72450",
          "301826",
          "72194",
          "397186",
          "73554",
          "375938",
          "73206",
          "1812515",
          "72k",
          "397k"
        ],
        "trecho": "Four neural baselines cover the dominant radio-map families under an identical-treatment fairness contract (Table~\\ref{tab:baseline_arch}). RadioUNet~\\cite{levie2021radiounet} stands in for the convolutional family with a U-Net encoder--bottleneck--decoder, skip connections ($256 \\to 128 \\to 64 \\to 128 \\to 256$), and \\num{265602}~parameters. REM-GNN is an in-house baseline implemented for this study, a lightweight MLP over the shared node features with two 256-unit hidden layers (\\num{72450}~parameters). RadioGAT~\\cite{li2024radiogat} brings attention via a dual terrain-plus-link encoder proje"
      },
      {
        "linha": 419,
        "tokens": [
          "1812515"
        ],
        "trecho": "The ablation asks whether the message-passing graph, rather than parameter capacity, carries the accuracy of GNN-RF. A capacity-matched control replaces the architecture with a multilayer perceptron holding exactly \\num{1812515} trainable parameters and no message passing, trained with the same loss, partition, checkpoint rule, and epoch budget on the eight cells of Lins and Bauru with five seeds each (40~paired runs). A second arm keeps the trained seed-42 models and damages the graph at test time on the same eight cells, with three shuffling seeds per arm: antenna-to-terrain edges are permut"
      },
      {
        "linha": 516,
        "tokens": [
          "Size"
        ],
        "trecho": "\\caption{Modern baseline architectures under the identical-treatment protocol of the earlier campaign (96~runs). Parameters counted on the trained Bauru checkpoints; the other cities add a terrain-to-terrain message-passing step. Size assumes 4 bytes per parameter.}"
      },
      {
        "linha": 520,
        "tokens": [
          "Params",
          "Size"
        ],
        "trecho": "Model & Params & Size & Family & Key mechanism \\\\"
      },
      {
        "linha": 522,
        "tokens": [
          "265602"
